      "method": "v1.1.0 case-law rebuild: authorities selected per decision point, each opinion retrieved from CourtListener and read; holding and quote taken from the opinion text; independently re-verified before release. Previous keyword-matched Justia listings were removed.",
      "verified_date": "2026-09-20",
      "key_cases": [
        {
          "case_name": "In re T.R.P.",
          "citation": "360 N.C. 588, 636 S.E.2d 787 (2006)",
          "court": "Supreme Court of North Carolina",
          "year": 2006,
          "date_decided": "2006-11-17",
          "holding": "The Supreme Court held that verification of a juvenile petition under N.C.G.S. § 7B-403(a) is a jurisdictional prerequisite, so an unverified petition leaves the district court without subject matter jurisdiction and every order entered in the case is void ab initio.",
          "relevance": "Verification of the petition is the jurisdictional gateway to opening a dependency case in North Carolina.",
          "quote": "the district court could not exercise subject matter jurisdiction here in the absence of the verification",
          "url": "https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/6833535/in-re-trp/",
          "courtlistener_cluster_id": 6833535
        },
        {
          "case_name": "In re A.P.",
          "citation": "371 N.C. 14, 812 S.E.2d 840 (2018)",
          "court": "Supreme Court of North Carolina",
          "year": 2018,
          "date_decided": "2018-05-11",
          "holding": "The Supreme Court held that the Juvenile Code does not confine the filing of an abuse, neglect, or dependency petition to the director of the county department of social services where the juvenile resides or is found, so a petition filed by another county director does not defeat subject matter jurisdiction.",
          "relevance": "It defines who has standing to file the petition that opens a dependency case in North Carolina.",
          "quote": "the statute does not identify which county director of social services must file the petition",
          "url": "https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/4497360/in-re-ap/",
          "courtlistener_cluster_id": 4497360
        },
        {
          "case_name": "In re K.C.G.",
          "citation": "171 N.C. App. 488, 615 S.E.2d 76 (2005)",
          "court": "Court of Appeals of North Carolina",
          "year": 2005,
          "date_decided": "2005-07-19",
          "holding": "The Court of Appeals upheld the ex parte order directing the mother to cease interfering with the DSS investigation but held that the trial court lacked jurisdiction to award sole temporary custody to the father because no nonsecure custody request had been made and no abuse, neglect, or dependency petition had been filed.",
          "relevance": "The petition is the jurisdictional precondition to any custodial order in a juvenile case.",
          "quote": "a request for nonsecure custody must be made and a petition must be filed before the court may issue an order for nonsecure custody",
          "url": "https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/7473109/in-re-kcg/",
          "courtlistener_cluster_id": 7473109
        },
        {
          "case_name": "In re R.T.W.",
          "citation": "359 N.C. 539, 614 S.E.2d 489 (2005)",
          "court": "Supreme Court of North Carolina",
          "year": 2005,
          "date_decided": "2005-07-01",
          "holding": "The Supreme Court held that a trial court retains jurisdiction to enter an order terminating parental rights while an appeal of a custody order in the same juvenile case is pending, rejecting the Court of Appeals' contrary rule and explaining that N.C.G.S. § 7B-1003 expands rather than limits trial court jurisdiction in child custody proceedings.",
          "relevance": "It explains how jurisdiction over a dependency case, once properly obtained by petition, coexists with the separate jurisdictional grant governing termination proceedings.",
          "quote": "we hold a trial court retains jurisdiction to terminate parental rights during the pendency of a custody order appeal in the same case",
          "url": "https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/6832153/in-re-rtw/",
          "courtlistener_cluster_id": 6832153
        }
      ],
